mpvpaper: sleep while there is nothing to do

Three loops woke a hundred times a second whatever the player did, a paused
or hidden one included (about 300 wakeups a second and 0.7 % of a core for a
wallpaper that doesn't move): the render loop polled its two descriptors with
a 10 ms timeout, the mpv event thread asked for an event without waiting then
slept 10 ms, and the auto-pause thread slept 10 ms at a time while hidden.

Everything the render loop does comes from its two descriptors (Wayland
events, mpv's "frame ready" eventfd), so it now waits on them (a pass a second
as a safety net; while held, the hold's deadline keeps the 10 ms check), and
quitting wakes it through the eventfd. The event thread waits in
mpv_wait_event (a second at most: the slideshow timer, the loop's end). The
auto-pause thread looks for the frame callback every 50 ms while hidden.

--- a/src/main.c
+++ b/src/main.c
@@ -139,6 +139,9 @@
 
     // Give mpv a chance to finish
     halt_info.stop_render_loop = 1;
+    // The render loop sleeps until something happens: tell it
+    uint64_t wake = 1;
+    if (wakeup_fd > 0 && write(wakeup_fd, &wake, sizeof(wake)) < 0) { /* its next pass sees it */ }
     for (int trys=10; halt_info.stop_render_loop && trys > 0; trys--) {
         usleep(10000);
     }
@@ -417,7 +420,7 @@
             update_mpv_pause_state(&halt_info.auto_paused, true, "Frame Callback");
 
             while (!halt_info.frame_ready) {
-                pthread_usleep(10000);
+                pthread_usleep(50000);
             }
             if (halt_info.auto_paused)
                 update_mpv_pause_state(&halt_info.auto_paused, false, "Frame Callback");
@@ -464,7 +467,9 @@
